.TH CTPOOL_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ctpool_declare_scoped \- declare a thread pool variable with automatic RAII destruction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ctpool_declare_scoped(name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Works like ctpool_declare(), but also attaches a cleanup attribute, so
the pool destroys itself when the variable leaves scope, exactly as
ctpool_destroy() destroys it.

You must still assign the variable before you use it. If the variable is
still CTPOOL_INVALID at the end of scope, the cleanup does nothing.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  Variable name to declare.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
It needs GCC or Clang, because it uses __attribute__((cleanup)).
Run a shutdown yourself before the end of scope when the drain behavior
matters: the shutdown that the destroy runs by itself drains the queue,
and that drain has no bound on its duration when the tasks are long.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
{
    ctpool_declare_scoped(pool);
    pool = ccol_create_cthread_pool(4, 0, NULL);
    ctpool_submit(pool, my_fn, my_arg, NULL);
    ctpool_shutdown_drain(pool);
}  /* pool is destroyed automatically here */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_declare (3),
.BR ctpool_construct_scoped (3),
.BR ctpool_destroy (3)
